TITLE:: RLScore
summary:: The notes of a piece on one time line, and what analysts wrote over them
categories:: Libraries>reLation
related:: Classes/RLFit, Classes/RLRegions, Classes/RLScale, Guides/reLation

DESCRIPTION::
A score read from the JSON file that HomewareSC's code::Math/Category_Theory/fit/dcml_read.py:: writes from a corpus of annotated scores. Time is in quarter notes from the start. Notes are spelled (their place on the line of fifths), so the same score is read into any equal division with a fifth.

A note is an Event code::(onset:, dur:, pitch:, pc:, tpc:, staff:, mn:)::, its pitch in the system's steps from middle C. A chord written by an analyst is code::(onset:, dur:, mn:, label:, key:, root:, bass:, pcs:, type:, numeral:)::, its key a region code::[fifths, mode]:: from the piece's global tonic (link::Classes/RLRegions::), its root and tones pitch classes. Read the entries with code::[\key]::, not as messages.

code::
~score = RLScore.read(path);
~score.chords.first[\label];
~score.regions;                       // RLRegions round the global tonic
~score.coverage(0, 8, [5, 8, 0]);     // [covered, total]: quarter notes of what sounds in the span
~fit = RLFit(~score.chords, ~score.regions);
::

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: read
argument:: path
argument:: system
An link::Classes/RLEDO:: (12 steps when nil).

METHOD:: new
A score from its parts.

METHOD:: pitchOf
A spelled note (MIDI number and place on the line of fifths) as a pitch of an equal division.

INSTANCEMETHODS::

METHOD:: notes
METHOD:: chords
METHOD:: marks
Cadences and phrase marks: code::[onset, measure, cadence, phrase mark]::.
METHOD:: measures
METHOD:: info
METHOD:: system
METHOD:: duration

METHOD:: tonic
METHOD:: regions

METHOD:: sounding
The notes sounding in a span, each with the part of it inside.
METHOD:: weights
Pitch class to quarter notes sounded in a span.
METHOD:: coverage
How much of what sounds in a span is one of some pitch classes: code::[covered, total]::.

METHOD:: measureAt
METHOD:: chordAt
METHOD:: between
The part of the score between two measures.
